{"id": "reflection_lessons_local_backend", "issue_ref": "#467", "base_commit": "42a38c91", "prompt": "## Slice C — Reflection lessons local backend\nPart of #462.\n\n### Context\nReflection's propose→gatekeep→persist flow stores lessons/questions via `src/core/intelligence/reflection_lessons_store.py`; the GUI reads/answers via `reflection_commands.py` → `window.pywebview.api.{get_reflection_lessons,set_reflection_lesson_status,answer_reflection_question}`. Dedup already local; only persistence is cloud-bound.\n\n### Scope\n1. `register_table` for the lessons/questions store (mirror columns: `id, kind, content, status, confidence, question, answer, created_at, ...`).\n2. Ensure `reflection_lessons_store` reads/writes go through the local shim when Supabase is unconfigured.\n3. Verify GUI review panel (get / approve / dismiss / answer) works offline end-to-end.\n\n### Definition of Done\n- [ ] No `SUPABASE_URL`: a reflection cycle proposes a lesson → appears in GUI queue → approve/dismiss/answer persists — all local.\n- [ ] Pending lessons survive a Brain restart.\n- [ ] `tests/unit/test_reflection_store_local.py` parity test.\n- [ ] No Supabase error logs when unconfigured.\n\n### Files owned\n`src/core/intelligence/reflection_lessons_store.py`.", "fail_to_pass": ["pytest tests/unit/test_reflection_store_local.py"], "pass_to_pass": ["pytest tests/unit/test_reflection_dedup.py"], "timeout_s": 180}
{"id": "telegram_routing_state_local", "issue_ref": "#471", "base_commit": "344188cb", "prompt": "## Slice G — Telegram routing-state localization (partial)\nPart of #462.\n\n### Context\nTelegram is inherently a cloud service — the bot API + webhook require internet and a bot token, which can never be local. What CAN be local is its routing state: the chat/session mapping in `telegram_chats` via `src/infrastructure/repositories/telegram_chat_repository.py`. This slice localizes only that state and documents the boundary.\n\n### Scope\n1. `register_table(\"telegram_chats\", ...)` and route the repo through the local shim when Supabase is unconfigured.\n2. Confirm chat-id to session routing persists + resolves offline (a locally-configured bot token works without Supabase).\n3. Docs: state that Telegram transport stays cloud; only routing state is local.\n\n### Definition of Done\n- [ ] No `SUPABASE_URL` but a bot token set: inbound Telegram messages route to the right session using local state.\n- [ ] Routing state survives a Brain restart.\n- [ ] `tests/unit/test_telegram_chats_local.py` parity test.\n- [ ] Boundary documented (external transport vs local state).\n\n### Files owned\n`src/infrastructure/repositories/telegram_chat_repository.py`.", "fail_to_pass": ["pytest tests/unit/test_telegram_chats_local.py"], "pass_to_pass": [], "timeout_s": 180}
{"id": "gate_lmstudio_adapter_workarounds", "issue_ref": "#498", "base_commit": "0c303ec2", "prompt": "Part of epic #450 (Model Provider Freedom).\n\n## Vision\nA pile of LM-Studio-specific inference workarounds fire today based on ad-hoc URL/name detection rather than declared provider capabilities. For an OSS user on Ollama / llama.cpp / vLLM these hacks are at best dead weight and at worst wrong. Make them declarative capability flags that are verified-off for non-LM-Studio backends.\n\n## Known LM-Studio-specific hacks to gate\nAll currently keyed on `self.provider == \"LM Studio\"` (URL-detected in `openai_adapter.py`) or model-name sniffing:\n- Qwen3 json_schema on the reasoning channel — json_schema binds to the reasoning channel leaving content empty; Chad disables thinking when tools+Qwen3+LM Studio.\n- `tool` -> `user` role transform with `<tool_response>` tags for Qwen3 froggeric template.\n- Froggeric fixed-template dirty tool-call parser.\n- gpt-oss Harmony `reasoning_effort` via extra_body.\n- `api_key` defaulting to `lm-studio`.\n\n## Goal\n1. Add a provider capability concept declaring per-provider flags: `needs_qwen3_reasoning_workaround`, `needs_tool_role_transform`, `has_native_tool_parser`, `reasoning_effort_via_extra_body`, etc.\n2. Replace the scattered `provider == \"LM Studio\"` / name checks in `openai_adapter.py` + `litellm_adapter.py` with capability lookups keyed on the resolved provider + model family.\n3. Ensure every flag resolves False/off for Ollama, vLLM, llama.cpp unless explicitly needed. Add tests asserting the workarounds do NOT fire for non-LM-Studio providers.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- [ ] No `== \"LM Studio\"` string comparisons remain in the adapters; all gated via capability lookups.\n- [ ] Tests prove each workaround fires for LM Studio + is off for Ollama/vLLM/llama.cpp.\n- [ ] LM Studio behavior unchanged (existing adapter tests green).", "fail_to_pass": ["pytest tests/unit/test_provider_capabilities.py"], "pass_to_pass": ["pytest tests/unit/test_provider_lifecycle.py"], "timeout_s": 180}
{"id": "issue_516", "issue_ref": "#516", "base_commit": "1c01629d3398908254eb21cfa29338ccb5de0014", "prompt": "Part of epic #512.\n\n## What\nAdd a cache layer that catches exact or near-repeat queries (e.g. \"what's on my calendar today\" asked twice in a session, or semantically-similar rephrasing) and skips inference entirely, or at minimum skips the embedding/RAG retrieval step.\n\n## Why this matters (research-backed)\nStandard production pattern is a multi-layer cache stack: **semantic cache (100% savings on repeats) \u2192 prefix cache (50-90% savings, already shipped in PR #511) \u2192 full inference** ([Introl](https://introl.com/blog/prompt-caching-infrastructure-llm-cost-latency-reduction-guide-2025), [Synthorai](https://synthorai.io/blog/llm-prompt-caching-complete-guide/)). PR #511 covered the prefix layer; this issue is the layer above it. For \"it just works when I ask\" \u2014 where the same handful of requests (status checks, routine lookups) recur \u2014 this is close to a free win.\n\n## Suggested approach\n1. Start narrow: cache at the query-embedding level (you already compute embeddings for RAG retrieval \u2014 reuse them for a similarity check against a short-lived cache of recent Q\u2192A pairs).\n2. Scope to side-effect-free requests only (never cache anything that triggers a tool with real-world effects) \u2014 the idempotency/dedup logic already in `ToolCoordinator` is a useful reference for how \"safe to skip\" is currently reasoned about.\n3. Short TTL to start (minutes, not hours) \u2014 correctness risk is higher than the prefix-cache change, so ship conservatively and measure hit rate before expanding scope.\n\n## Effort / executor\n**Low-medium** \u2014 contained, mostly a new cache module wired into the selector/retrieval path. Suggested executor: **Sonnet**.\n\n## Blocked by / parallel-safety\n- Not blocked by anything. Touches `src/infrastructure/tools/selection/llm_selector.py` and/or the RAG retrieval path \u2014 low overlap with other epic #512 issues.\n- Safe to run in parallel with everything except issues that also touch the selector (none currently do).", "fail_to_pass": ["pytest tests/unit/test_semantic_cache.py", "pytest tests/unit/test_semantic_cache_turn_wiring.py"], "pass_to_pass": ["pytest tests/unit/test_config_manager.py"], "timeout_s": 300}
{"id": "issue_513", "issue_ref": "#513", "base_commit": "3a08c6f47f4f661fb72e011264f73323ae6cd2a1", "prompt": "Part of epic #512.\n\n## What\nThe supervisor currently delegates to one specialist agent (`delegate_to_code_agent`, `delegate_to_browser_agent`, etc.) at a time and waits. When a user's ask decomposes into independent sub-tasks (e.g. \"check my email AND look up the weather AND summarize this doc\"), those delegations could fire concurrently instead of sequentially.\n\nThis is the same underlying idea as PR #511 (parallel tool calls) but one level up \u2014 parallel **delegation**, not just parallel primitive-tool calls. Claude Code's own architecture does exactly this: \"distributing tasks across five agents costs almost the same as running one sequentially\" because they share a prompt cache ([hidekazu-konishi.com](https://hidekazu-konishi.com/entry/claude_code_subagents_and_orchestration_guide.html), [claudefa.st](https://claudefa.st/blog/guide/agents/sub-agent-best-practices)). There's also a paper specifically on this shape for small models \u2014 \"Small Model as Master Orchestrator: Learning Unified Agent-Tool Orchestration with Parallel Subtask Decomposition\" ([arXiv 2604.17009](https://arxiv.org/html/2604.17009)) \u2014 worth a skim before design.\n\nMatthew has confirmed LM Studio serves up to 8 concurrent 9B requests without crashing on his hardware, so the capacity is real \u2014 it's currently unused.\n\n## Why this matters here\nChad's `handoff` tools (`delegate_to_*`) are themselves tool calls \u2014 so PR #511's persona change (\"batch independent calls in the same turn\") should already make the model *emit* multiple delegate calls together sometimes. But the execution side needs verification: does `ToolCoordinator.execute_batch`'s `asyncio.gather` actually run two `delegate_to_*` handoffs concurrently today, or does the LangGraph handoff/subgraph mechanism serialize them internally (e.g. via a shared checkpoint lock)? That's the first thing to check before building anything new.\n\n## Prerequisite check (do this first, cheap)\n**LM Studio's `Max Concurrent Predictions` setting defaults to 4, not 8** ([LM Studio docs](https://lmstudio.ai/docs/app/advanced/parallel-requests)). If sub-agents share one loaded model instance, requests 5-8 will silently queue behind the cap instead of running concurrently. Verify/raise this setting for whichever model the specialists share before assuming 8-way fan-out is actually happening end-to-end.\n\n## Suggested approach\n1. Confirm the LM Studio concurrency cap (above).\n2. Trace whether `execute_batch`'s `gather` already parallelizes concurrent `delegate_to_*` calls, or whether LangGraph's subgraph/checkpoint machinery serializes them.\n3. If serialized: identify the lock/checkpoint boundary and design around it (may need per-delegation sub-state instead of a single shared checkpoint).\n4. Add an eval/probe measuring wall-clock for a multi-delegation turn before/after (mirror `scripts/ttft_cache_probe.py`'s before/after pattern).\n\n## Effort / executor\n**Medium-high** \u2014 architecture-sensitive, touches the handoff/graph machinery. Suggested executor: **Opus** (needs judgment on LangGraph checkpoint semantics, not mechanical).\n\n## Blocked by / parallel-safety\n- Not blocked by anything merged. Builds on PR #511 (parallel tool calls) \u2014 rebase onto it once merged.\n- **Touches**: `src/core/orchestration/handoffs.py`, `src/core/orchestration/nodes/supervisor_node.py`, `src/core/orchestration/agents/specialist_graph.py`, `src/core/orchestration/coordinators/tool_coordinator.py`, model-lifecycle/concurrency config.\n- **Overlaps with** issue \"Plan-DAG / LLMCompiler pattern\" (also touches supervisor_node.py + tool_coordinator.py) \u2014 don't run both at once; do this one first since Plan-DAG subsumes it.", "fail_to_pass": ["pytest tests/unit/test_parallel_delegate_fanout.py"], "pass_to_pass": ["pytest tests/unit/test_config_manager.py"], "timeout_s": 300}
{"id": "issue_478", "issue_ref": "#478", "base_commit": "decb2f8be0998ae1123ca577d542acf3d4d69245", "prompt": "## Problem\n\nFound while re-running Google-dependent live suites after fixing OAuth (see #476). `agent_lane_complex_live` hung for 30+ minutes with zero progress after the `agent_multi_mcp_chain_drive_email` case, well past when the Brain itself had already completed that turn (18:05:08 in `data/prod_logs/brain_20260710.log` -- it hit a `create_gmail_draft` `tool_approval_request` and returned). The eval runner process itself never advanced to the next case.\n\n## Two distinct issues\n\n1. **`evals/live.py:41`**: `RESPONSE_TIMEOUT = int(os.getenv(\"EVAL_WORKFLOW_ROUND_TIMEOUT_S\", \"3600\"))` -- a 1-hour default meant for heavy coding workflow rounds -- is reused as-is for `probe_live` (`evals/live.py:173-176`), the plain (non-workflow) live-lane single-turn probe. Any live case that stalls has up to a full hour before the harness even times it out and records `\"no final response within {RESPONSE_TIMEOUT}s\"`. There should be a separate, much shorter timeout for the plain live lane (workflow rounds legitimately need up to an hour for heavy coding; a single live turn does not).\n\n2. **No fast-path for approval/clarification pauses in `probe_live`.** `probe_workflow` (`evals/live.py:200+`) explicitly detects `awaiting_approval`/`awaiting_input` via `PAUSE_REASONS` and auto-advances with a canned `approval` response. `probe_live` has no equivalent -- it just calls `_collect` once and blindly waits for `RESPONSE_TIMEOUT`. Cases like `destructive_action_requires_approval` (0/1 in the #413 scoreboard) exercise exactly this gap.\n\n3. **Whatever caused the actual hang here** was neither of the above -- the Brain-side turn completed fine at 18:05:08, but the eval runner's own process (PID tree under `evals.runner --suite agent_lane_complex_live --live --judge ...`) never logged progress to the next case for 30+ minutes afterward, with no CPU/network activity visible. Likely stuck in judging or result-serialization for a trace that ended on a `tool_approval_request` shape rather than a normal final response. Needs a repro + probably a targeted timeout around the judge call and/or defensive handling of that trace shape.\n\n## Impact\n\nUnattended overnight/multi-hour sweeps (see #413's `run_sweep_413.ps1`) can silently burn up to an hour per stuck case with zero results, and a manual targeted rerun (this session) hung for 30+ min without even hitting the 1-hour ceiling -- meaning there's a hang path that isn't even bounded by `RESPONSE_TIMEOUT`.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\n- Add a separate, short timeout (e.g. 60-120s) for `probe_live`'s single-turn wait, distinct from the workflow round timeout.\n- Give `probe_live` the same `PAUSE_REASONS` detection `probe_workflow` has, and either auto-resolve (decline/approve per a case-level default) or fail fast with a clear \"case requires approval, cannot complete unattended\" error instead of waiting for a final response that will never come via that path.\n- Investigate the actual runner-process hang after `eval_live_7a6cc7ad` (2026-07-10 ~18:05-18:36 CDT) -- add a bounded timeout around judging/serialization so one bad trace can't stall an entire suite indefinitely.", "fail_to_pass": ["pytest tests/unit/test_lmstudio_gate.py"], "pass_to_pass": ["pytest tests/unit/test_config_manager.py"], "timeout_s": 300}
{"id": "issue_497", "issue_ref": "#497", "base_commit": "4c385f1a62cf7408cdc6acb4e68cea56adf4ae5e", "prompt": "Part of epic #450 (Model Provider Freedom). Strategy: `docs/strategy/OPEN_SOURCE_STRATEGY.md`.\n\n## Vision\nInference is **already** provider-agnostic (`LiteLLMAdapter` / `OpenAIAdapter`, `LocalModelDiscovery` enumerates LM Studio **and** Ollama). The *remaining* hard lock-in is the **lifecycle layer**: `model_swap.py` and `model_lifecycle.py` drive LM Studio's **native, non-OpenAI** API \u2014 `GET/POST /api/v1/models/load|unload` keyed by `instance_id` (see `src/core/intelligence/model_swap.py:50-131`). No other backend speaks that:\n- **Ollama** \u2014 models load on first request; you control residency with `keep_alive` (`keep_alive: 0` to evict), no instance IDs.\n- **llama.cpp server / vLLM** \u2014 serve a **single** resident model; there is **no** load/unload API at all. Load/unload must be a no-op.\n\nSo JIT load, the heavy-model double-load guard, and the subagent model-swap dance are all LM-Studio-shaped.\n\n## Goal (this issue \u2014 the hard architectural core; do FIRST)\nIntroduce a **provider lifecycle abstraction** so every load/unload/residency decision dispatches through a per-provider strategy, with **zero per-provider branching leaking into orchestration** (epic scope 1 + 2).\n\n## Approach\n1. New module `src/core/intelligence/providers/` (or `provider_lifecycle.py`) defining a `ProviderLifecycle` Protocol:\n   - `list_loaded(base_url) -> list[str]` (OpenAI-compat `/v1/models` \u2014 already generic)\n   - `load(base_url, model_id, context_length=None) -> bool`\n   - `unload(base_url, model_id) -> bool`\n   - `supports_unload: bool` / `supports_explicit_load: bool` capability flags\n2. Implementations:\n   - `LMStudioLifecycle` \u2014 lift the current native `/api/v1/models/load|unload` + `resolve_instance_id` logic verbatim from `model_swap.py` (no behavior change for LM Studio).\n   - `OllamaLifecycle` \u2014 load = warm request or `/api/generate` with `keep_alive`; unload = `keep_alive: 0`. Verify against real Ollama.\n   - `StaticEndpointLifecycle` (llama.cpp/vLLM) \u2014 `load`/`unload` are no-ops returning True; residency is whatever the server started with.\n3. Provider selection: resolve from `local_models.providers` config by matching `base_url` (LM Studio `:1234`, Ollama `:11434`, else `static`) OR an explicit `provider:` key per endpoint. Centralize the base_url\u2192provider mapping \u2014 no scattered `\"1234\" in url` checks (there are several today, e.g. `openai_adapter.py:35`).\n4. Refactor `model_swap.py` to be a **thin fa\u00e7ade** delegating to the resolved provider; keep `_clean_model_id` / `_same_model` shared helpers. Refactor `model_lifecycle.py`'s `ensure_resident`/`release` to call the provider strategy instead of `model_swap` primitives directly. The `_BaseUrlState`, VRAM budget, per-base_url serialization, and interactive-active signal all stay \u2014 they're provider-agnostic already.\n5. The heavy-model double-load guard (`model_swap.load_model` idempotency, ref: memory `lmstudio-heavy-model-double-load`) must be preserved for LM Studio and be a safe no-op for static endpoints.\n\n## Research / open questions\n- Confirm exact Ollama residency control that works through its OpenAI-compat proxy at `:11434/v1` vs its native `:11434/api/*` \u2014 may need the native path for `keep_alive`.\n- Decide whether `StaticEndpointLifecycle` should still honor VRAM-budget eviction (probably skip: single model, nothing to evict).\n- `llm_selector.py` has a parallel **sync** unload/instance-resolution path (see `model_swap.py` docstring) \u2014 note it for a follow-up so it doesn't drift; may be out of scope here.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- [ ] `model_swap.py` / `model_lifecycle.py` contain **no** hardcoded `/api/v1/models/...` LM-Studio paths \u2014 those live only in `LMStudioLifecycle`.\n- [ ] Provider chosen from config/base_url; adding a new backend = one new `ProviderLifecycle` impl, no orchestration edits.\n- [ ] LM Studio behavior byte-for-byte unchanged (existing tests + a manual swap smoke pass).\n- [ ] Static-endpoint provider makes load/unload safe no-ops (llama.cpp/vLLM don't crash the lifecycle coordinator).\n- [ ] Unit tests for provider selection + each impl's load/unload contract (mock HTTP).\n\n## Executor / coordination\n- **Executor: Opus** \u2014 architectural refactor of the 683-line `model_lifecycle.py` + `model_swap.py`; touches the production model-load path, needs care.\n- **Blocked-by:** none (foundational). **Blocks:** #450b, #450d.\n- **Owns files:** `model_lifecycle.py`, `model_swap.py`, new `providers/` module. Parallel-safe with #450c (wizard/UI). Coordinate `local_models.providers` schema (adds optional `provider:` key) with #450c.\n\nRefs #450, #219.\n", "fail_to_pass": ["pytest tests/unit/test_provider_lifecycle.py"], "pass_to_pass": ["pytest tests/unit/test_config_manager.py"], "timeout_s": 300}
{"id": "issue_472", "issue_ref": "#472", "base_commit": "9e61c83432a20ed6c9f3a4aca47ff7e85fb5633a", "prompt": "## Slice H \u2014 Local-parity tests + docs + Settings surface\nPart of #462. \u00b7 **Blocked by: B\u2013G.** \u00b7 **Executor: Sonnet.**\n\n### Context\nFinal slice: prove the epic end-to-end, update sovereignty docs, and make local-vs-cloud status visible in the app.\n\n### Scope\n1. `tests/integration/test_local_first_subsystems.py` \u2014 with `SUPABASE_URL` unset, exercise each localized subsystem (cron, reflection, kanban, workflows, dashboard, OATS) through its real repo, restart-simulate (reconnect sqlite), assert state persists.\n2. Update `docs/setup/LOCAL_FIRST.md` \u2014 flip \"requires Supabase\" matrix rows to \"local\" for every subsystem that landed; keep Telegram as \"state local / transport cloud\"; keep multi-user auth as Supabase-only.\n3. Settings \u2192 Services surface (`get_services_health` / Services panel): show each feature as \"Local (SQLite)\" vs \"Cloud (Supabase)\".\n\n### Definition of Done\n- [ ] Integration test green with no `SUPABASE_URL` across all landed subsystems, incl. restart-survival.\n- [ ] `LOCAL_FIRST.md` matrix updated + accurate.\n- [ ] Settings/Services shows per-feature local/cloud status.\n- [ ] Epic #462 acceptance criteria all checkable.\n\n### Files owned\n`tests/integration/test_local_first_subsystems.py` (new), `docs/setup/LOCAL_FIRST.md`, Settings/Services panel + `get_services_health` path.\n\n### Parallel-safety\nLast slice. Depends on B\u2013G; scaffold once \u22651 consumer lands, finalize after all land.\n\nRefs #462.\n", "fail_to_pass": ["pytest tests/integration/test_local_first_subsystems.py", "pytest tests/unit/test_services_health_endpoint.py"], "pass_to_pass": ["pytest tests/unit/test_config_manager.py"], "timeout_s": 300}
{"id": "issue_470", "issue_ref": "#470", "base_commit": "c7d8eeaa44b66d1f8bcfeda1192a05b113386e84", "prompt": "## Slice F \u2014 OATS local refinement (vector)\nPart of #462. \u00b7 **Blocked by: Slice A.** \u00b7 **Executor: Opus** (vector math + refiner correctness + multi-writer outcome log).\n\n### Context\nOATS self-improves tool selection by refining tool embeddings from logged outcomes. Two Supabase tables:\n- `tool_outcome_log` \u2014 written from **several** places (`src/infrastructure/tools/event_wrapper.py`, `src/core/intelligence/capability_patterns.py`, `src/core/intelligence/reflection.py`, `src/remote/endpoints.py`).\n- `tools` \u2014 base + refined embeddings, read/written by `scripts/oats_refine.py` (`refine_embeddings`), triggered by `src/core/orchestration/oats_refiner.py`.\nRefinement math is already numpy/local (`l2_normalize`, weighted centroids); only the data lives in the cloud. Mirrors the memories vector pattern solved locally in `local_store.py` (#416).\n\n### Scope\n1. `register_table(\"tool_outcome_log\", ...)`; ensure the outcome-log write path in `event_wrapper.py` (+ other writers) persists locally when unconfigured.\n2. Local source for the `tools` catalog embeddings (`embedding`, `embedding_refined`, `oats_*`). If the local tool-RAG already stores tool embeddings, refine in place; otherwise register a `tools` shim table.\n3. `refine_embeddings()` currently early-returns when `db.enabled is False` \u2014 redirect it to run against the local store.\n4. Confirm `OATSRefiner.increment_and_check` \u2192 background refine works offline.\n\n### Definition of Done\n- [ ] No `SUPABASE_URL`: tool outcomes log locally \u2192 after N outcomes, `refine_embeddings` runs on local data and writes `embedding_refined` locally.\n- [ ] Refined embeddings **survive a Brain restart** and are picked up by the selector.\n- [ ] `tests/unit/test_oats_local.py` parity test (extend existing `test_oats*.py`).\n- [ ] No Supabase error logs when unconfigured.\n\n### Files owned\n`scripts/oats_refine.py`, `src/core/orchestration/oats_refiner.py`, `src/infrastructure/tools/event_wrapper.py` (outcome-log write path only), `tests/unit/test_oats_local.py` (new). Coordinate with A on the `rpc`/vector handler.\n\n### Parallel-safety\nOwns `tool_outcome_log` + `tools` (OATS columns). Independent of B\u2013E, G. P1 after A. Note: `event_wrapper.py` is a hot path \u2014 keep the change to the outcome-log write only.\n\nRefs #462.\n", "fail_to_pass": ["pytest tests/unit/test_oats_local.py"], "pass_to_pass": ["pytest tests/unit/test_config_manager.py"], "timeout_s": 300}
{"id": "issue_466", "issue_ref": "#466", "base_commit": "35be286870e02d286fb15d353980cb1ba697f432", "prompt": "## Slice B \u2014 Cron / agent_tasks local backend\nPart of #462. \u00b7 **Blocked by: Slice A.** \u00b7 **Executor: Sonnet.**\n\n### Context\nScheduled/agent tasks persist to `agent_tasks` via `src/infrastructure/repositories/task_repository.py`; `src/core/scheduling/cron_scheduler.py` polls for due tasks and recovers stuck ones. Plain CRUD + a \"due before now\" query.\n\n### Scope\n1. `register_table(\"agent_tasks\", ...)` mirroring current Supabase columns (`id, name, schedule, next_run, status, payload, created_at, updated_at, ...`).\n2. Confirm every `task_repository` method works through the shim unchanged. If a query uses grammar A doesn't cover, report it upstream to A \u2014 don't fork logic.\n3. Verify scheduler \"find due\" (`next_run=lte.<now>`), `create_task`, status transitions, `_recover_stuck_tasks` (`status=eq.running` reset) run offline.\n\n### Definition of Done\n- [ ] No `SUPABASE_URL`: create a task \u2192 persists \u2192 scheduler fires when due \u2192 status updates \u2014 all local.\n- [ ] Tasks + `next_run`/status **survive a Brain restart**.\n- [ ] `_recover_stuck_tasks` resets running tasks locally.\n- [ ] `tests/unit/test_cron_local.py` parity test (create/list-due/update/recover).\n- [ ] No Supabase error logs when unconfigured.\n\n### Files owned\n`src/infrastructure/repositories/task_repository.py`, `src/core/scheduling/cron_scheduler.py`, `tests/unit/test_cron_local.py` (new).\n\n### Parallel-safety\nOwns `agent_tasks` + scheduler. Disjoint from C\u2013G. P1 after A.\n\nRefs #462.\n", "fail_to_pass": ["pytest tests/unit/test_cron_local.py"], "pass_to_pass": ["pytest tests/unit/test_config_manager.py"], "timeout_s": 300}
{"id": "issue_468", "issue_ref": "#468", "base_commit": "6893c2c46118a52aa6dcae275b64228a7c431353", "prompt": "## Slice D \u2014 Kanban + Workflows local backend (CRUD twins)\nPart of #462. \u00b7 **Blocked by: Slice A.** \u00b7 **Executor: Cursor** (mechanical template-following CRUD, tight blast radius).\n\n### Context\nTwo near-identical plain-CRUD repos:\n- `src/infrastructure/repositories/kanban_repository.py` \u2014 table `kanban_cards` (list/get/create/update/move/delete).\n- `src/infrastructure/repositories/workflow_repository.py` \u2014 table `workflows` (list/create/update/delete).\nBoth should work through the shim with **near-zero code** \u2014 mostly schema registration + tests.\n\n### Scope\n1. `register_table(\"kanban_cards\", ...)` and `register_table(\"workflows\", ...)` with current columns.\n2. Confirm every repo method works unchanged via the shim; add local handling only if a query uses unsupported grammar (report upstream to A).\n3. Kanban `move_card` state-machine validation is pure-Python (`work_status.py`) \u2014 unaffected.\n\n### Definition of Done\n- [ ] No `SUPABASE_URL`: create/list/move a kanban card and create/list/run a workflow \u2014 all local.\n- [ ] Cards + workflows **survive a Brain restart**.\n- [ ] `tests/unit/test_kanban_local.py` and `tests/unit/test_workflows_local.py` parity tests.\n- [ ] No Supabase error logs when unconfigured.\n\n### Files owned\n`src/infrastructure/repositories/kanban_repository.py`, `src/infrastructure/repositories/workflow_repository.py`, `tests/unit/test_kanban_local.py` + `tests/unit/test_workflows_local.py` (new).\n\n### Parallel-safety\nOwns `kanban_cards` + `workflows`. Disjoint from B, C, E\u2013G. P1 after A.\n\nRefs #462.\n", "fail_to_pass": ["pytest tests/unit/test_kanban_local.py", "pytest tests/unit/test_workflows_local.py"], "pass_to_pass": ["pytest tests/unit/test_config_manager.py"], "timeout_s": 300}
{"id": "issue_465", "issue_ref": "#465", "base_commit": "ea72bb2650ee780cde1c6ee0f5a0e167a147fbb5", "prompt": "## Slice A \u2014 LocalPostgrestShim foundation\nPart of #462. **Blocks B\u2013H.** \u00b7 **Executor: Opus** (cross-cutting, singleton wiring, grammar translation, correctness-critical).\n\n### Context\nEvery Supabase-only subsystem calls `SupabaseManager` (`src/infrastructure/database/supabase_manager.py`) with PostgREST-style verbs + filters. When `enabled is False` today, every verb returns empty/None (silent no-op). This slice makes the disabled path **delegate to a local SQLite adapter** so the same repo code transparently works offline \u2014 the exact pattern `memory_repository.py` hand-rolls, generalized once.\n\n### Scope\n1. New `src/infrastructure/database/local_postgrest.py` \u2014 `LocalPostgrestShim` implementing `query/insert/update/upsert/delete` (sync) + a filter-grammar translator:\n   - operators: `eq.`, `neq.`, `gt.`, `gte.`, `lt.`, `lte.`, `is.null`, `ilike.*x*`, `like.`, `in.(...)`\n   - `or=(a,b,...)` clause; `order` (`col.asc/desc`, multi-col); `limit`; `offset`; `select` (passthrough); `on_conflict` for upsert.\n2. Backing tables in `data/local_chad.db` (reuse `local_store.local_db_connect()`); auto-created lazily from a registered schema map. Each consumer slice declares its table via `register_table(name, columns, pk)` so the shim never needs per-feature edits.\n3. Wire into `SupabaseManager`: when `not self.enabled`, route the five verbs to the shim. `rpc` raises `NotImplementedError` unless a handler is registered (OATS slice F registers its vector match).\n4. Preserve return shapes exactly (`list[dict]` query, `dict|None` insert/upsert, `bool` update/delete).\n\n### Definition of Done\n- [ ] `local_postgrest.py` with verbs + filter-grammar translator.\n- [ ] `SupabaseManager` disabled-path delegates to the shim for all five verbs.\n- [ ] `register_table` API + lazy `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS`.\n- [ ] `tests/unit/test_local_postgrest.py`: each operator, `or=()`, multi-col order, limit/offset, upsert-on-conflict, full insert\u2192query\u2192update\u2192delete round-trip.\n- [ ] Enabled path (Supabase configured) provably untouched \u2014 assertion test.\n- [ ] Zero new required deps.\n\n### Files owned\n`src/infrastructure/database/local_postgrest.py` (new), `src/infrastructure/database/supabase_manager.py`, `tests/unit/test_local_postgrest.py` (new). Ref: `local_store.py`, `memory_repository.py`.\n\n### Parallel-safety\nFoundation. **Blocks B\u2013H.** Solo \u2014 nothing else touches `supabase_manager.py` concurrently.\n\nRefs #462, #448.\n", "fail_to_pass": ["pytest tests/unit/test_local_postgrest.py"], "pass_to_pass": ["pytest tests/unit/test_config_manager.py"], "timeout_s": 300}
{"id": "issue_363", "issue_ref": "#363", "base_commit": "bb2e5fdb6656328f1aa67c31ce7c9959b07d35b7", "prompt": "## Summary\r\n\r\nAdd a **Settings \u2192 Services** panel in the Chad GUI that shows health/status of Chad's infrastructure dependencies, plus a unified `docker/chad-services.yml` for optional self-hosted sidecars. Separately clarify in UI/docs the distinction between **Services** (Chad infrastructure) and **MCPs** (optional tool add-ons).\r\n\r\n## Problem\r\n\r\nChad is accumulating dependencies that are hard for users to discover and verify:\r\n\r\n| Kind | Example | User pain |\r\n|------|---------|-----------|\r\n| **Service** | SearXNG `:8080`, self-hosted Kroki, Typst CLI | \"Is search working?\" \"Why did my diagram fail?\" |\r\n| **MCP add-on** | RenderLens, excalidrawer, 2Slides | \"Do I have this MCP?\" \"Which tool needs it?\" |\r\n| **Blurred** | Web search (core behavior, SearXNG service + Brave fallback) | Core feature backed by optional infra |\r\n\r\nToday setup is scattered across `config.yaml`, docker folders, and docs. Not braindead easy.\r\n\r\n## Design principles\r\n\r\n### Services vs MCPs (communicate clearly)\r\n\r\n**Services** \u2014 infrastructure Chad (or a Chad tool) calls directly. Belong in the Services panel.\r\n\r\n- Examples: Brain `:8767`, LM Studio `:1234`, SearXNG `:8080`, self-hosted Kroki, Typst/Marp CLIs on PATH\r\n- Tiered honestly:\r\n  - **Core** \u2014 Chad is broken or severely degraded without these (Brain, LM Studio in local mode)\r\n  - **Enhanced** \u2014 core features work with fallback or degraded mode (SearXNG \u2192 Brave; public Kroki \u2192 self-host)\r\n  - **Optional** \u2014 nice-to-have capabilities (self-hosted Kroki for privacy, Presenton, RenderLens server)\r\n\r\n**MCPs** \u2014 optional add-on tool servers discovered at boot. Belong in a separate **MCP Add-ons** section (or tab), **linked to the native tools/skills they power**.\r\n\r\n- Examples: RenderLens MCP \u2192 animated SVG QA; excalidrawer \u2192 whiteboard diagrams; future presentation MCPs\r\n- Never imply MCPs are required for basic Chad operation\r\n- Show: MCP name \u2192 tools enabled \u2192 setup link \u2192 connected/disconnected status\r\n\r\n### Association rules (UI copy)\r\n\r\n- Each **service** card: name, tier badge, health, what breaks if down, one-click setup hint\r\n- Each **MCP** card: name, **\"Powers: `render_diagram`, `web_search`, \u2026\"**, install/config snippet, prewarm status from `MCPScanner`\r\n- When a tool is selected and its backing service/MCP is down, supervisor or UI should surface a actionable message (not a generic tool error)\r\n\r\n## Proposed UX: Settings \u2192 Services\r\n\r\n### Services tab\r\n\r\nStatus cards with green/yellow/red:\r\n\r\n| Service | Health check | Tier |\r\n|---------|--------------|------|\r\n| Brain | `GET /health` on configured URL | Core |\r\n| LM Studio | `GET /v1/models` | Core (local mode) |\r\n| SearXNG | `GET :8080` or search smoke | Enhanced (Brave fallback) |\r\n| Kroki | `POST` smoke or version endpoint; skip if using public URL | Enhanced (public fallback) |\r\n| Typst CLI | `typst --version` | Enhanced (PDF tool) |\r\n| Marp CLI | `marp --version` or npx probe | Enhanced (slides tool) |\r\n\r\nActions per card:\r\n- **Verify now** (re-ping)\r\n- **Setup** \u2014 guided command or open docs (`winget install`, `docker compose up`, etc.)\r\n- **Configure** \u2014 deep-link to relevant `config.yaml` keys (`tools.searxng_url`, `visual.kroki_url`, \u2026)\r\n\r\n### MCP Add-ons tab (or subsection)\r\n\r\n- List MCP servers from `config.mcp_servers` + discovery status\r\n- For each: connected tools (from registry), last error, link to enable/disable\r\n- Distinguish visually from Services (addon badge vs infrastructure badge)\r\n\r\n### Unified Docker (optional self-host stack)\r\n\r\nSingle compose file, e.g. `docker/chad-services.yml`:\r\n\r\n```yaml\r\n# Optional sidecars \u2014 not required for first-run\r\nservices:\r\n  searxng: ...\r\n  kroki: ...      # optional; public kroki.io works without this\r\n  # presenton: ...  # phase 2\r\n  # renderlens: ... # phase 3\r\n```\r\n\r\nGUI button: **Start optional services** \u2192 `docker compose -f docker/chad-services.yml up -d`\r\n\r\nPowerShell helper: `scripts/setup/verify_chad_services.ps1` (callable from GUI and CI smoke)\r\n\r\n## Implementation sketch\r\n\r\n### Backend (Brain or Limb)\r\n- `GET /api/services/health` \u2014 aggregate pings (async, timeout per service)\r\n- Reuse existing config paths; no secrets in response\r\n- MCP status from existing `MCPScanner` / registry stubs\r\n\r\n### Frontend (Limb React)\r\n- New Settings section: **Services & Add-ons**\r\n- Service cards + MCP cards with tier badges and tool associations\r\n- Optional: tray indicator when Enhanced tier is degraded (e.g. SearXNG down, using Brave)\r\n\r\n### Docs\r\n- Short `docs/guides/SERVICES_AND_MCPS.md` \u2014 taxonomy, tiers, fallbacks\r\n- Update CLAUDE.md pointer only if needed\r\n\r\n## Out of scope (this issue)\r\n\r\n- Auto-install Docker Desktop / winget packages (guided commands only)\r\n- Merging all MCPs into native tools\r\n- Presenton/RenderLens implementation (separate issues #350, #351)\r\n\r\n## Acceptance criteria\r\n\r\n- [ ] User can open Settings and see Core/Enhanced/Optional service health at a glance\r\n- [ ] MCP add-ons listed separately with **which tools they power**\r\n- [ ] SearXNG + optional Kroki in unified compose; public Kroki documented as zero-setup default\r\n- [ ] Degraded mode explained in UI when fallback is active (e.g. \"Search: using Brave fallback\")\r\n- [ ] `verify_chad_services.ps1` script matches GUI checks\r\n\r\n## Size estimate\r\n\r\n~L (GUI panel + health API + compose + docs). Could split: (1) health API + script, (2) GUI panel, (3) compose file.\r\n\r\n## Related\r\n\r\n- Phase 1 visual tools: PR #362 (Kroki/Typst/Marp)\r\n- #351 RenderLens MCP, #350 Presenton, #354 excalidrawer MCP\r\n- `config.tools.searxng_url`, `config.visual.kroki_url`\r\n", "fail_to_pass": ["pytest tests/unit/test_tool_catalog_source.py"], "pass_to_pass": ["pytest tests/unit/test_app_controller.py"], "timeout_s": 300}
{"id": "issue_328", "issue_ref": "#328", "base_commit": "8db726c3fef8b060e3c829409a41fa345d102878", "prompt": "## Context\n\nIssue #279 (PR #322) added computer-use safeguards for `browser_agent`'s Playwright calls: a per-site allowlist (`tools.browser_write_allowlist` in `config/config.yaml`) and write-action classification driving the existing approval-gate. Issue #325 (PR #326, #327) fixed the propagation bug so the approval actually pauses the turn and surfaces a card.\n\nNone of this is visible or configurable from the GUI today \u2014 it's config-file only. Raised during live testing of #325/#327: \"where are the allowlisted sites surfaced and configurable on the gui, and where are the modes like allow everything, let model decide, and ask everything.\"\n\n## Scope\n\n1. **Allowlist editor** \u2014 a Settings surface (likely alongside the recently-added `SubagentsTab.tsx` / `SettingsView.tsx`, per the `feature/audio-hardware` merge that touched those files) to view/add/remove domains in `tools.browser_write_allowlist` without hand-editing `config/config.yaml`. Needs a read/write API path \u2014 check whether `config/config.yaml` edits already have a settings-write plumbing (e.g. how `subagent_model_overrides` gets written from the Subagents tab) and reuse that pattern rather than inventing a new one.\n2. **Approval mode selector** \u2014 three modes, config-driven (new key, e.g. `tools.browser_approval_mode`), read by `browser_safety.classify()`:\n   - **Allow everything** \u2014 writes execute without pausing (fully autonomous; for a trusted/sandboxed session).\n   - **Let model decide** (current default behavior) \u2014 the existing write/read + allowlist classification in `src/core/orchestration/browser_safety.py`.\n   - **Ask everything** \u2014 every browser_* action (including currently-auto reads like `browser_navigate`/`browser_snapshot`) pauses for approval.\n3. Surface the mode + allowlist together in one settings section so they read as one coherent \"browser safety\" control, not two unrelated toggles.\n\n## Not in scope / already done\n\n- The approval-gate mechanics themselves (pause, ApprovalCard, propagation through specialist subagents) \u2014 done in #279/#325.\n- Per-action write classification heuristics \u2014 done in `browser_safety.py`, tune separately if the mode selector reveals classification issues.\n\n## Size\n\nM \u2014 mostly React (new settings section + API wiring) plus a small `browser_safety.py` change to branch on the new mode key.\n\nRefs: #279, #325.", "fail_to_pass": ["pytest tests/unit/test_browser_safety_modes.py"], "pass_to_pass": ["pytest tests/unit/test_model_lifecycle.py"], "timeout_s": 300}
{"id": "issue_283", "issue_ref": "#283", "base_commit": "32bcd05a776b4e9df2ba9806b0d300dae11b115d", "prompt": "## Problem\r\n\r\nThe heartbeat reflection cycle (`propose_lessons()` \u2192 `LessonGatekeeper` \u2192 `persist_lesson_and_publish()`) surfaces duplicate proposed lessons in the GUI \u2014 e.g. multiple pending rows for **\"The user's name is Matthew\"** at ~95% confidence, even though that fact already lives in Chad's knowledge graph / `personal_knowledge.md`.\r\n\r\nThis is two distinct failures:\r\n\r\n1. **The reflection judge is blind to existing knowledge** \u2014 it only sees recent conversation log + tool failures (`load_reflection_log()`). It does not see the knowledge graph, `personal_knowledge.md`, persona `## Learned Lessons`, or pending `reflection_lessons` queue rows. Conversations that mention \"Matthew\" repeatedly cause the model to keep proposing an obvious fact as novel.\r\n\r\n2. **No dedup against the review queue** \u2014 unlike `capability_gaps.py` (which loads `existing_pending_titles()` into `SuggestionGatekeeper`), `reflection_lessons_store.persist_lesson_and_publish()` always `INSERT`s. Each 24h heartbeat can add another identical pending row. Dismissed rows do not block re-proposal.\r\n\r\nPost-hoc guards exist but are incomplete:\r\n\r\n- `LessonGatekeeper` token-dedup only checks persona `tools` `## Learned Lessons` \u2014 not graph facts, not pending queue (`user` scope relies on graph dedup instead).\r\n- `_drop_graph_duplicates()` (threshold 0.62) can miss short lesson text vs. bloated identity section nodes; degrades silently to admit-everything if embeddings unavailable.\r\n- No unit tests for graph dedup path.\r\n\r\nRelated: #194 (GUI review queue), design in `docs/DESIGN_reflective-lessons.md`.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Proposed fix\r\n\r\nReflection is off the hot path (daily heartbeat + thumbs-down dry-run). Cost of a false positive >> cost of extra tokens. **Do not trim context aggressively** \u2014 give the judge everything relevant for an informed abstain/skip decision.\r\n\r\n### 1. Upgrade reflection judge model\r\n\r\n- Change `reflection.model` default from `qwen/qwen3.5-9b` to a capable local model (e.g. **Gemma** or other loaded LM Studio model \u2014 pin in config).\r\n- Design doc always recommended a larger model for lessons than for tool selection; lessons persist and compound.\r\n\r\n### 2. Fat context bundle for the judge (Mem0/Hermes pattern)\r\n\r\nBefore proposing, inject an **ALREADY KNOWN** block into the reflection prompt (single pass, or pass 2 of a two-pass flow):\r\n\r\n| Source | Purpose |\r\n|--------|---------|\r\n| `personal_knowledge.md` and/or all valid graph node texts | Canonical user facts |\r\n| Pending + recent `reflection_lessons` rows (`pending`, optionally `applied`/`dismissed`) | Stop GUI duplicates + inform judge |\r\n| Persona `## Learned Lessons` (tools record) | Operational lessons already filed |\r\n| Live tool catalog snapshot | Judge-aware dead-tool context |\r\n| Full conversation log (remove or raise `log[:24000]` truncation) | Pattern detection |\r\n| Recent tool failures | Grounding (existing) |\r\n\r\nPrompt instruction (adapt from Mem0 v3 extraction rubric):\r\n\r\n- *\"If a candidate lesson is semantically equivalent to an item in ALREADY KNOWN with no meaningful new context, do not emit it.\"*\r\n- *\"Entity mentioned in existing memory \u2260 all facts about that entity captured \u2014 only skip when the specific fact itself is already covered.\"*\r\n\r\nOptional two-pass: (1) propose from history, (2) novelty-filter candidates against full memory context.\r\n\r\n### 3. Queue-aware deterministic dedup (mirror `capability_gaps.py`)\r\n\r\n- Add `existing_pending_lesson_texts(supabase)` \u2014 texts from `reflection_lessons` where `status IN (pending, shown)` (consider `applied`/`auto_applied` for cross-cycle dedup).\r\n- Pass into `LessonGatekeeper(existing_lessons=...)` alongside persona lessons.\r\n- Guard `persist_lesson_and_publish()` with Jaccard check before insert (belt + suspenders).\r\n\r\n### 4. Strengthen graph cross-store dedup (optional / follow-up)\r\n\r\n- Log loudly when `graph_dedup_active=False` (currently silent pass-through).\r\n- Consider EvolveR-style gray zone: embedding similarity 0.45\u20130.62 \u2192 cheap LLM equivalence YES/NO.\r\n- Unit tests: `\"The user's name is Matthew\"` vs identity section blob; pending queue dedup; gatekeeper + graph chain.\r\n\r\n### Files likely touched\r\n\r\n- `src/core/intelligence/reflection.py` \u2014 context builder, prompt, remove log truncation\r\n- `src/core/intelligence/lesson_gatekeeper.py` \u2014 document that `existing_lessons` includes queue + graph summaries\r\n- `src/core/intelligence/reflection_lessons_store.py` \u2014 `existing_pending_lesson_texts()`, insert guard\r\n- `src/core/monitoring/heartbeat_service.py` \u2014 wire context if needed\r\n- `config/config.yaml` \u2014 `reflection.model`\r\n- `tests/unit/test_lesson_gatekeeper.py`, new graph/queue dedup tests\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Research: what other agent harnesses do (and why)\r\n\r\nIndustry pattern is **not** \"smarter proposer prompt alone.\" Production systems use layered dedup:\r\n\r\n```\r\nRetrieve existing state \u2192 inject into proposer \u2192 instruct to skip equivalents\r\n  \u2192 deterministic write guard \u2192 periodic consolidation (later)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Diff/UPDATE approaches are largely retired** \u2014 Mem0 v2 used two LLM calls (extract + ADD/UPDATE/DELETE against existing memories); v3 moved to single-pass ADD-only with retrieved context because diffing was slow and error-prone.\r\n\r\n### OpenClaw\r\n\r\n- **Canonical store:** plain Markdown (`MEMORY.md`, `memory/YYYY-MM-DD.md`) \u2014 \"files are source of truth.\"\r\n- **Search:** SQLite hybrid index (embeddings + BM25) built *from* those files \u2014 not instead of them.\r\n- **Dedup:** MMR re-ranking on `memory_search` results (Jaccard diversity); optional Dreaming promotion gate for long-term memory; agent can `memory_search` before writing.\r\n- **Why md:** human-editable, local-first, auditable, no infra dependency. Embeddings are a derived index.\r\n\r\n### Hermes (Nous Research)\r\n\r\n- **Canonical store:** `MEMORY.md`, `USER.md`, skills as `SKILL.md` \u2014 frozen into system prompt every session so the model always sees current memory.\r\n- **Write guard:** `memory` tool `add` rejects exact duplicates (\"Entry already exists\").\r\n- **Skills GC:** Curator background pass archives stale skills; optional LLM consolidation merges overlapping near-duplicates.\r\n- **Why md:** transparency, user sovereignty, prompt-cache-friendly frozen snapshots; DB used for session search (`state.db` FTS), not as sole memory truth.\r\n\r\n### Mem0 v3\r\n\r\n- **Flow:** Retrieve top-10 related existing memories \u2192 single LLM call with `## Existing Memories` section \u2192 \"semantically equivalent \u2192 skip\" \u2192 MD5 exact dedup \u2192 ADD + entity linking.\r\n- **Explicitly abandoned** v2 diff/UPDATE/DELETE (two LLM calls).\r\n- **Philosophy:** duplicates may accumulate at write time; hybrid retrieval ranks at read time \u2014 but proposer still sees existing memories first.\r\n\r\n### EvolveR / EvolveMem / AutoRefine / MEGA (research)\r\n\r\n- **EvolveR:** embedding retrieve similar principles \u2192 **LLM binary equivalence judgment** \u2192 merge under existing or add novel.\r\n- **EvolveMem:** Jaccard dedup on consolidation pass + importance decay.\r\n- **MEGA:** hash exact dedup \u2192 LLM conflict detection \u2192 embedding similarity merge.\r\n- Common theme: cheap similarity first, precise judgment second, never blind propose.\r\n\r\n### Voyager\r\n\r\n- Key-based overwrite (function name), not semantic dedup \u2014 different problem (executable skills). Near-duplicates with different names can coexist.\r\n\r\n### Chad today vs field\r\n\r\n| Layer | Industry norm | Chad today |\r\n|-------|---------------|------------|\r\n| Proposer sees existing memory | Mem0, Hermes, OpenClaw | \u274c conversation log only |\r\n| Proposer sees pending proposals | Chad `capability_gaps` does this | \u274c |\r\n| Skip instruction in prompt | Mem0 explicit | \u274c generic \"abstain\" only |\r\n| Write-time queue dedup | Hermes exact, capability_gaps Jaccard | \u274c always INSERT |\r\n| Periodic consolidation | Hermes Curator, OpenClaw Dreaming | \u274c (design doc: later) |\r\n\r\n### Why markdown + DB hybrid (not either/or)\r\n\r\nChad's architecture is already aligned with the field:\r\n\r\n- **`personal_knowledge.md` / graph** \u2014 human-editable canonical facts (you edit; graph re-seeds)\r\n- **`persona_config` / `## Learned Lessons`** \u2014 versioned operational lessons in prompt\r\n- **`reflection_lessons` (Supabase)** \u2014 workflow queue (pending \u2192 approved \u2192 applied), not durable memory\r\n\r\nMarkdown/graph = what Chad **knows**. Supabase queue = what Chad is **proposing** to learn. Embeddings = what machines **search**. The bug is the reflection judge isn't wired to read any of the canonical stores before proposing.\r\n\r\nRefs: `docs/DESIGN_reflective-lessons.md`, `docs/architecture/MEMORY_AND_CONTEXT.md`, Mem0 v3 migration guide, Hermes Curator docs, OpenClaw memory docs.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Acceptance criteria\r\n\r\n- [ ] Heartbeat reflection does not surface duplicate pending lessons for facts already in graph or already pending (e.g. user name).\r\n- [ ] Reflection judge prompt includes ALREADY KNOWN context (graph and/or personal knowledge, pending queue, persona lessons).\r\n- [ ] `existing_pending_lesson_texts()` wired into gatekeeper and/or insert guard (pattern from `capability_gaps.py`).\r\n- [ ] `reflection.model` upgraded to capable local judge (Gemma or documented alternative).\r\n- [ ] Unit tests for queue dedup and graph-dedup edge cases.\r\n- [ ] `evals/runs/reflect_proposal_latest.md` shows `already-in-graph dropped` / rejected duplicates when re-run against known-fact history.\r\n\r\n## Size estimate\r\n\r\nMedium \u2014 ~1 session. Core work is context builder + queue dedup; model swap is config + validation.\r\n", "fail_to_pass": ["pytest tests/unit/test_reflection_dedup.py"], "pass_to_pass": ["pytest tests/unit/test_model_lifecycle.py"], "timeout_s": 300}
